  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nEarly this morning water vapor satellite along with upper air \nanalysis showed an upper trough moving onshore across the west \ncoast. The main upper jet extended around the upper trough along the\nwest coast, northeast across the northern Plains, then northeast \ninto eastern Canada. A broad upper ridge was centered across the \nSoutheast US, with an upper ridge axis extending west-southwest \nacross TX into southern NM. Broad southwesterly mid level flow was \nobserve across KS, with max 500mb wind speeds of 20 KTS.\n\nThe 6Z surface map showed a surface cold front extending from the \neastern Great Lakes southwest, southwest across southern IL, central\nMO, far southeast KS, then into the southern TX PNHDL. Showers and \nelevated thunderstorms continue to develop northwest of the surface \nfront across eastern KS. However, the intensity of the thunderstorms \nhave diminished. There could still be periods of heavier rainfall \nthrough the mid morning hours across northeast and north central KS.\n\n\nToday:\n\nThe H5 ridge across the southeast US will retrograde westward into \nthe lower MS River Valley by 00Z THU. The larger scale isentropic \nascent will gradually shift northeast of the CWA during the late \nmorning hours. Therefore, the rain showers and elevated \nthunderstorms should shift northeast of the CWA by this afternoon. \nSkies should clear through the afternoon hours across the southern\ncounties and high temperatures will warm into the lower to mid 90s \nacross east central KS as the H5 ridge axis build northward across \nsoutheast KS/central MO. The front will be retreating northward but \nthe northern counties will be mostly cloudy with northeast winds and \ncooler temperatures, with highs in the mid to upper 70s. The upper \nlow across the western US will amplify across northern CA. \n\nTonight through Friday night:\n\nThe upper low over northern CA will shift east into the Great Basin \nby Friday afternoon. The H5 ridge will amplify across the southern \nPlains and eastern KS into southern MO. Expect warm and dry weather. \nHighs will warm into the mid to upper 80s north and lower to mid 90s \nnorth. Heat indices will climb to around 100 degrees during the \nafternoon hours of Thursday and Friday across east central KS with \nheat indices in the 90s elsewhere across the CWA.\n\nSaturday through Sunday:\n\nThe extended range models are in good agreement with lifting the \nGreat Basin H5 trough northeast into the northern and central high \nPlains Saturday night, then into the northern Plains late Sunday. A \nstronger cold front will move through the area Saturday night into \nSunday. There will be a chance for showers and thunderstorms \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. North central KS may see \nthunderstorms as early as Saturday afternoon. Saturday looks to be \nour last warm summer like day, with highs in the upper 80s to mid \n90s southeast of the front across east central KS. Highs will be in \nthe lower 80s across the northern counties of the CWA.\n\nOn Sunday there may be lingering post frontal showers across the \neastern half of the CWA. Highs wil be cooler with lower 70s north \nand lower 80s across the southeast counties of the CWA.\n\n\nSunday night through Wednesday: \n\nThe extended range models forecast cooler temperatures with \nhighs in the upper 60s to lower 70s north and mid to upper 70s \nsouth. An H5 trough will move onshore across southern CA Monday \nnight and fill as it lifts northeast across the central Plains \nthrough midweek. The ascent ahead of the H5 trough may provide \nchance for scattered showers and a few elevated thunderstorms \nTuesday and Wednesday.",
